I've seen a bit of buzz online about  something called the Fineman technique  and how you can use it to improve your  grades  well the Fineman technique is  essentially the idea that if you can  explain a concept in simple language and  perhaps to someone else and answer their  questions about it then it's really good  practice for your own learning and  understanding  I think the technique is vaguely named  after richard fineman because this is  sort of an idea that he spoke about one  of his quotes is that if you can't  simply explain an idea then you probably  don't understand it well enough although  fine one would probably be surprised to  have this idea named specifically after  him because it is kind of a common-sense  idea anyone can sort of see that if  you're able to explain a concept that's  going to help you make sure you know it  yourself I don't think we need to be so  uptight about the exact steps to learn  through the Fineman technique but there  are definitely ways that we can  practically implement it into our own  learning one of them is simply just  talking to other people if you're a  student probably at university then just  talking to your peers and classmates  about the idea that you're learning try  to study with them is great because what  you can also ask your questions you can  answer theirs I don't think that as  students we should be shy of asking our  peers and friends questions on on how  something works or how to do a certain  part of an assignment because you might  feel guilty that you're taking up their  time and if it but really you are sort  of helping them as well as long as it's  not too over the top I think they would  appreciate putting into practice  explaining something they know  so I would say don't be shy to ask  people for help because it's going to  help the both of you another way you can  probably implement this technique is  just posting on forums whether it's  physics forums or any other type of  internet space there's always people  asking very intellectual or curious  questions about every topic and if you  think you're an expert in something or  you're studying to become an expert in  something go and check out some of the  relevant questions online and you may be  surprised to find some of the gaps and  your knowledge  I think answering questions on those  sites is really helpful it puts  knowledge out there to the world but it  also like I said helps you find out what  you don't understand I know I definitely  appreciate the people that post on those  kind of forums because they've helped me  many times in the past and I like to  think that they also gain some benefit  from answering these questions this  whole idea of explaining something  simply mimics a technique I've heard of  called rubber duck debugging which is  used  I think widely in coding circles because  what it is is essentially having a  little rubber duck or a toy or something  on your desk and when you've reached a  point in your task where you don't know  why it's not working you don't know why  your code seems to have a bug or why  something's going wrong instead of just  staring at the code for hours on end and  getting nowhere  you try to explain to the rubber duck  how the code works how every line of the  code is working and usually through  doing that you pick up on the mistake  yourself you realize that something in  there doesn't quite make logical sense  and it's a way to debug your own  programs it's all I think a similar idea  and like I said it's kind of common  sense  you don't really need a certain label to  this technique but I think it's good to  keep in mind that as well as always  trying to ask questions make sure you're  trying to answer them as well because  both of those components are going to  make you a really good learner and  someone that really understands what  they're trying to learn
 

